7.1 The data
Almost every real dataset has missing values. By default, most R functions handle them with listwise deletion: any case missing a variable in the model is dropped. This can shrink the sample a great deal, and, if the missing cases differ from the others, it can bias the results. In this chapter, we explore patterns of missing data, ask why the data might be missing, and use multiple imputation to keep every case in the analysis.
The data come from a 2004 survey of 4,508 Oregon residents about community and environmental health. Our outcome is a scale score for concern about the environment (env_con). The predictors are:
Income is recorded in dollars, so a one-unit change is one dollar. We rescale it to units of $10,000, which makes its coefficient readable. We also drop the ID variable, which we do not need and should not be used to impute other values.
oregon &lt;- read_dta("data/oregon2004.dta") |&gt;
  zap_labels() |&gt;
  mutate(inc = inc / 10000) |&gt;
  select(-ID)

describe(oregon, fast = TRUE)
Notice that n differs across variables. That is the first sign of missing data.

8.4 Describing the clusters
The final step is to describe each cluster in the original units of the variables. We also compare the clusters on SES, which was not used to form them.

els_cluster_clean |&gt;
  mutate(cluster = factor(km$cluster)) |&gt;
  group_by(cluster) |&gt;
  summarize(
    n = n(),
    across(c(homework, engage, importance, ses),
           ~ round(mean(.x, na.rm = TRUE), 2))
  )






A plot of the standardized centers shows each cluster’s profile:

as_tibble(km$centers) |&gt;
  mutate(cluster = factor(row_number())) |&gt;
  pivot_longer(-cluster, names_to = "variable", values_to = "mean") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = variable, y = mean, fill = cluster)) +
  geom_col(position = "dodge") +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Mean (standard deviation units)", fill = "Cluster")








The three clusters can be described roughly as:

Engaged: above average on math engagement and importance, with typical homework time.
High homework: far above average on homework time, with average views of math.
Less engaged in math: well below average on math engagement and importance.

These labels are our interpretation. Naming clusters is a judgment call, and labels should describe the profile without overstating what it means. The SES means differ somewhat across clusters, though a formal test (for example, an ANOVA) would be needed before drawing conclusions.

9.1.1 Reverse coding
Most items are worded so that agreeing indicates more anxiety (for example, Q01: “Statistics makes me cry”). Q03, “Standard deviations excite me,” is worded in the opposite direction. A correlation plot shows the problem: Q03 correlates negatively with most of the other items.

cor(saq) |&gt;
  corrplot(method = "color", type = "upper", tl.cex = 0.7)








We reverse-code Q03 by subtracting it from 6 (one more than the highest response) and drop the original:

saq_clean &lt;- saq |&gt;
  mutate(Q03R = 6 - Q03) |&gt;
  select(-Q03)
